{
  "summary": "This study investigates strategies to stabilize aqueous zinc-iodine batteries by inhibiting polyiodide formation, a key limitation. Using a site-separated polymer (SSP) binder, the researchers selectively confined iodine and iodide ions at distinct functional groups, disrupting polyiodide generation pathways. The SSP cathode achieved high capacity retention (97.1% over 31,000 cycles) and superior performance at high currents and iodine loadings compared to conventional binders.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SSP binder selectively confines I2 and I- at different functional groups to disrupt polyiodide formation",
    "I2/SSP cathode retains 97.1% capacity over 31,000 cycles at 20 A g-1",
    "At 50 mg cm-2 iodine loading, SSP cathode maintains 10.1 mAh cm-2 with 97.9% retention over 400 cycles"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The site-separated polymer binder provides a fundamental mechanism to suppress polyiodide shuttling in aqueous zinc-iodine batteries, enhancing long-term stability and high-rate performance without compromising capacity."
}

{
  "summary": "This phase I trial evaluated intravesical CD40 agonist 2141-V11 in BCG-unresponsive non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer patients. The study assessed safety, preliminary clinical activity, and spatial immune correlates using multiplex spatial profiling. Results showed a 40% clinical response rate with median duration of 20 months. Responders exhibited distinct tertiary lymphoid structures with reduced DC-Treg proximity, while non-responders had increased DC-Treg proximity, indicating spatial immune organization as a predictor of response.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Intravesical 2141-V11 achieved a 40% clinical response rate with median response duration of 20 months",
    "Responders displayed reduced proximity between dendritic cells and regulatory T cells",
    "Non-responders showed increased dendritic cell-regulatory T cell proximity"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This study identifies spatial immune organization as a key determinant of response to CD40 agonist therapy in bladder cancer, providing a mechanistic basis for predicting clinical outcomes and guiding future immunotherapeutic strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether brief dietary changes during acute inflammation influence long-term tissue immune memory. Using lean mice with acute type 17 skin inflammation, researchers found that a transient high-fat diet (HFD) exposure within a 4-day window at disease onset exacerbates inflammation to levels seen in chronic obesity. HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the window prevents amplification. Mechanistically, HFD recruits neutrophils expressing Nlrp3 and Il1b, which license pathogenic TH17 cells. After recovery, chromatin-poised tissue-resident memory T cells persist, causing exaggerated inflammation upon recurrence despite subsequent LFD. The findings demonstrate that short-lived dietary states can durably reprogram immune responses when coinciding with tissue inflammation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Transient HFD exposure within a 4-day window at disease onset exacerbates acute skin inflammation to chronic obesity levels",
    "HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to LFD during the window prevents inflammatory amplification",
    "Neutrophil-derived Nlrp3-Il1b signaling licenses pathogenic TH17 cells, leading to persistent tissue-resident memory T cells"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes that transient dietary states during acute inflammation can durably reprogram immune memory, revealing a critical temporal window for dietary interventions to modulate inflammatory disease trajectories."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how human activities impact elephant social structures. Using habitat change analysis, it assessed behavioral shifts in endangered elephant populations. Results indicate significant disruption to social networks due to human-induced habitat fragmentation, threatening population viability. The findings highlight critical conservation challenges for elephant societies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Human-driven habitat fragmentation disrupts elephant social networks",
    "Endangered elephant populations face reduced social cohesion",
    "Habitat changes threaten elephant survival through social behavior impacts"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The research underscores the critical role of intact social structures in elephant conservation, emphasizing habitat protection as essential for maintaining viable populations."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the long-term stability challenges in perovskite-organic tandem solar cells (PO-TSCs) caused by halide phase segregation in wide-bandgap perovskite subcells. Using systematic aging and mechanistic analyses, the research identifies photothermal stress-induced halide phase segregation as the primary degradation pathway. The authors develop a halogenated cyclodextrin-based supramolecular strategy that stabilizes halide homogeneity through synergistic interactions, achieving 20.60% efficiency in 1.80-eV devices with T80 lifetimes of 1100 h under MPPT and T85 lifetimes of 1128 h under 85°C thermal stress.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Halide phase segregation in wide-bandgap perovskites under photothermal stress is the dominant degradation mechanism in PO-TSCs",
    "A halogenated cyclodextrin supramolecular strategy enables precise regulation of crystallization, defect passivation, and halide distribution",
    "The approach achieves 20.60% efficiency in 1.80-eV devices with T80 lifetimes of 1100 h under MPPT and T85 lifetimes of 1128 h under 85°C thermal stress"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a supramolecular design strategy for stabilizing wide-bandgap perovskites in tandem solar cells, addressing a critical stability bottleneck while maintaining high efficiency and flexibility."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates lactate's role in regulatory T cell (Treg) differentiation during sepsis. Using human sepsis patient data and murine models, researchers found plasma lactate correlates with Treg frequencies and disease severity. Lactate uptake via MCT1 increases histone lactylation and PRMT5 expression, driving symmetric dimethylation of Foxp3 at R51/R146. This stabilizes Foxp3, promoting Treg differentiation and suppressing immune responses, which exacerbates organ injury in sepsis. Targeting PRMT5 with dexmedetomidine reduced Treg accumulation and improved outcomes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Plasma lactate levels correlate with Treg frequencies and sepsis severity in patients",
    "Lactate uptake via MCT1 enhances histone lactylation and PRMT5-mediated Foxp3 symmetric dimethylation",
    "PRMT5 inhibition with dexmedetomidine reduces Treg accumulation and improves sepsis outcomes"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes lactate metabolism as a critical regulator of Treg differentiation through epigenetic mechanisms, providing a mechanistic link between metabolic dysregulation and immunosuppression in sepsis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the demographic processes underlying the spread of Celtic languages during the Iron Age using ancient genomic data from 4,587 individuals across Bronze and Iron Age Europe. By analyzing Steppe-related ancestry migration, a southwestern European to British movement, and a westward expansion from eastern central Europe, the research identifies three key population shifts relevant to Celtic dispersal. The findings suggest that the eastern central European migration (3200-2800 BP) most parsimoniously explains Celtic language spread, though linguistic and genetic histories may not align directly.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Steppe-related ancestry arrived via Bell Beaker groups around 4400 BP across Celtic-speaking regions",
    "Bronze Age migration from southwestern Europe to Britain occurred between 3800-3200 BP",
    "Westward expansion of eastern central European ancestry (Knovíz-Urnfield) affected France, the British Isles, northeastern Iberia, and Italy from 3200-2800 BP"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides genomic evidence for the most parsimonious demographic pathway in Celtic language dispersal across prehistoric Europe, clarifying the interplay between population movements and linguistic expansion."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether ESCRT machinery is required for ciliary vesicle budding. Using live-cell imaging and genetic perturbations in model organisms, researchers observed that vesicle budding occurs independently of ESCRT proteins. The findings indicate that ciliary vesicle formation can proceed without ESCRT, challenging previous assumptions about the role of this complex in membrane trafficking.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Ciliary vesicle budding occurs without ESCRT proteins",
    "ESCRT machinery is not essential for ciliary vesicle formation",
    "Vesicle budding from ciliary tips proceeds independently of ESCRT"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "These results demonstrate that ESCRT complexes are not universally required for membrane budding events, expanding understanding of the mechanisms governing ciliary membrane dynamics."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines design principles and engineering strategies for liquid-phase electrolytes in lithium metal batteries to overcome dendrite growth and interfacial instability. It details approaches like molecular architecture design, solvent-salt reconfiguration, additive incorporation, high-concentration electrolytes, and temperature-adaptive formulations. These strategies aim to enhance safety, voltage tolerance, fast charging, and thermal stability while addressing key technical barriers in Li metal anode implementation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Molecular architecture design of salt-solvent systems improves interfacial stability",
    "High-concentration electrolytes enhance dendrite suppression and thermal resilience",
    "Temperature-adaptive electrolyte formulations enable wide-temperature operation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a systematic framework for developing liquid-phase electrolytes that overcome critical limitations in lithium metal batteries, advancing the field toward practical high-energy-density applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how interleuk.2-inducible T-cell kinase (ITK) deficiency protects against pulmonary hemorrhage in mice. Using a pristane-induced model, researchers found ITK knockout mice showed near-complete resistance to pulmonary hemorrhage and multiorgan injury. Key mechanisms include reduced inflammatory cells, increased Foxp3+ regulatory T cells, and elevated amphiregulin production by Tregs. The findings suggest ITK modulates the balance between tissue damage and repair in autoimmune lung injury.",
  "key_findings": [
    "ITK deficiency conferred near-complete protection from pulmonary hemorrhage and multiorgan injury in mice",
    "ITK loss increased Foxp3+ regulatory T cells and amphiregulin production in the lungs",
    "Transcriptomic analysis revealed ITK deficiency reprogrammed Tregs toward enhanced metabolic and tissue-repair pathways"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies ITK as a critical regulator of the immune response balance in autoimmune pulmonary injury, providing a mechanistic basis for targeting ITK to promote tissue repair in severe inflammatory lung diseases."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates epigenetic heterogeneity in breast tumours, focusing on luminal subtypes. Using single-cell cytometry by time-of-flight, researchers identified a recurrent basal-like subpopulation within luminal tumours with a conserved epusing epigenetic signature. These cells exhibit aggressive oncogenic traits and reduced hormone receptor signaling. The findings suggest potential epigenetic liquid biopsies in blood for early diagnosis and prognosis, linking specific epigenetic markers to relapse risk.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Identification of a recurrent basal-like subpopulation in luminal breast tumours with a conserved epigenetic signature",
    "Basal-like cells show high expression of aggressive oncogenic traits and downregulation of hormone receptor signaling",
    "Epigenetic markers in blood correlate with disease relapse risk, enabling prognostic prediction"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study demonstrates that epigenetic heterogeneity within luminal breast tumours can be detected via single-cell profiling and linked to clinical outcomes, providing a foundation for epigenetic liquid biopsies in breast cancer diagnosis and prognosis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed a ratiometric near-infrared (NIR) sensing platform to monitor hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) in plants under environmental stress. The platform integrates an H2O2-sensitive aptamer-modified NIR fluorescent single-walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNTs), a stable NIR reference using polyethylene glycol-modified Egyptian Blue nanosheets (EB-NS), and a visible light signal for autofluorescence correction. It overcomes challenges like leaf motion and fluctuating light by enabling robust H2O2 detection with 75% higher signal contrast than single-channel methods, facilitating real-time in vivo monitoring of stress responses in field conditions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A ratiometric NIR sensing platform integrates H2O2-specific SWCNTs, a stable NIR reference (EB-NS), and a visible light signal for autofluorescence correction",
    "The platform achieves 75% higher signal contrast than single-channel detection under dynamic environmental conditions",
    "It enables continuous H2O2 monitoring despite leaf motion and fluctuating illumination, supporting field-deployable stress response assessment"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances interference-resistant optical sensing for plant stress monitoring, providing a scalable method for real-time H2O2 detection in natural environments without compromising signal accuracy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated hyperinsulinemia's role in regulatory T-cell dysfunction and disease activity in multiple sclerosis (MS). Researchers prospectively followed 131 relapsing-remitting MS patients stratified by BMI for 36+ months, assessing clinical outcomes, MRI activity, metabolic parameters, and Treg function at baseline. Mechanistic studies used insulin receptor signaling assays in Tregs. Results showed hyperinsulinemia correlated with reduced FoxP3, impaired Treg suppression, and increased inflammatory cytokines. Metformin treatment reduced insulin levels and improved Treg function, though causality could not be established due to the study design.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Hyperinsulinemia correlated with reduced FoxP3 and IL-10 in regulatory T-cells",
    "Insulin stimulation impaired Treg suppression via AKT-mTOR signaling",
    "Metformin treatment reduced fasting insulin and improved Treg function in obese MS patients"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes hyperinsulinemia as a modifiable factor linked to regulatory T-cell dysfunction in multiple sclerosis, providing a potential therapeutic target for mitigating disease activity through insulin signaling pathways."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates enhancing ultra-low-power 2D transistor performance using single-crystal high-κ dielectrics. Researchers integrated van der Waals-attached anatase titanium dioxide with 2D transition metal dichalcogenides to achieve a pristine interface. The approach yielded subthreshold swing near thermal limits, minimal hysteresis, and balanced n/p-type transistor characteristics. Complementary inverters demonstrated subpicowatt static power consumption, indicating significant potential for energy-efficient electronics.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Pristine 2D-dielectric interface enabled subthreshold swing close to thermal limit",
    "Record-low hysteresis and well-matched n/p-type transfer characteristics",
    "Complementary inverters achieved subpicowatt static power consumption"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes single-crystal high-κ dielectrics as a viable strategy for achieving near-ideal 2D transistor interfaces, advancing ultra-low-power electronics through minimized leakage and enhanced gate control."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates stretchable silicon solar cell modules using kirigami-structured substrates to address the brittleness of traditional silicon photovoltaics for wearable applications. The approach integrates commercial silicon cells with flexible kirig.ami substrates, enabling modules to withstand repeated deformations from body movements. Module-14-7S2P demonstrates 37.50 mW power output with >95% retention after 5000 cycles and >94% retention after 120 h water immersion, retaining ∼80% power at 13% strain. These results indicate enhanced durability for portable solar-powered wearables.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Module-14-7S2P achieves 37.50 mW power output",
    "Modules retain >95% power after 5000 deformation cycles",
    "Modules maintain >94% power retention after 120 h water immersion"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that kirigami-structured silicon solar cells can maintain high performance under mechanical stress, enabling practical solar power for wearable electronics without compromising durability."
}

{
  "summary": "This study evaluated an Ii-Key-modified HPV16/18 E7 epitope peptide vaccine in a murine TC-1 tumor model. The vaccine, designed to enhance MHC class II loading, induced dendritic cell maturation and T-cell activation, including increased CD4+ and CD8+ T-cell responses. It suppressed tumor growth and reduced Ki67 staining, with combination therapy using anti-PD-1 antibody showing superior tumor control compared to monotherapy. The approach demonstrates potential for improving HPV-targeted immunotherapy efficacy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Ii-Key-modified E7 peptides enhanced dendritic cell maturation and proinflammatory cytokine secretion",
    "Peptide immunization increased tumor-infiltrating CD4+ and CD8+ T-cell frequencies and reduced regulatory T cells in tumor-bearing mice",
    "Combination with anti-PD-1 antibody achieved greater tumor suppression than either treatment alone"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides preclinical evidence that Ii-Key-enhanced HPV E7 epitope vaccines can stimulate robust T-cell immunity and tumor control, offering a strategy to overcome antigen presentation limitations in cervical cancer immunotherapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the mechanistic disconnect between in vitro and in vivo NLRP3 inflammasome activation. It demonstrates that physiological extracellular matrix stiffness induces NRF2 expression, which is necessary for one-signal NLRP3 activation in human and mouse phagocytes. The research identifies Panx1 as an NRF2-regulated gene promoting NLRP3 activity. Macrophages from stiff bone-osteoclast matrices and pulmonary fibrosis patients show reduced NRF2 and impaired NLRP3 function. NRF2 also supports antitumour immunity via NLRP3 agonistic cancer vaccines.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Physiological ECM stiffness induces NRF2 expression required for one-signal NLRP3 activation",
    "Panx1 is an NRF2-regulated gene that promotes NLRP3 activity",
    "Macrophages from stiff bone-osteoclast matrices and pulmonary fibrosis patients exhibit reduced NRF2 and impaired NLRP3 function"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes NRF2 as a mechanosensitive transcriptional checkpoint that bridges in vitro and in vivo NLRP3 inflammasome activation, resolving a long-standing discrepancy in experimental models."
}

{
  "summary": "This review investigates degradation mechanisms in nickel-rich layered oxide (NRLO) cathodes by linking heterogeneous Li+ transport across atomic to macroscopic scales to phase transitions, microcracking, and surface reconstruction. It proposes 'transport homogenization' as a unifying design principle to mitigate degradation through bulk structural, interfacial, and dynamic strategies. The study establishes a framework for rational cathode design targeting high-energy-density batteries.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Heterogeneous Li+ transport across multiple length scales drives NRLO cathode degradation",
    "Transport homogenization unifies degradation mechanisms and enables rational design strategies",
    "Emerging characterization methods quantify transport heterogeneity for cross-scale design"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a mechanistic framework for understanding and mitigating degradation in high-energy-density nickel-rich cathodes by unifying transport heterogeneity with structural stability."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated an acid-responsive biomaterial designed for osteoporotic bone repair. The researchers created a composite implant using carboxymethyl chitosan-stabilized strontium carbonate integrated into synchronously mineralized collagen, covalently attached to PEEK. In acidic osteoporotic conditions, protonation of CMC accelerated strontium release, reprogramming macrophages to an M2 phenotype, increasing IL-1:0 production, suppressing osteoclastogenesis, and shifting immune balance toward regeneration. The implant enhanced bone formation in vivo, increasing bone volume fraction by 97.3% compared to unmodified PEEK in rabbit femoral defects at 8 weeks.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Acid-triggered strontium release from CMC-stabilized strontium carbonate in osteoporotic microenvironments",
    "Reprogramming of macrophages toward an M2 phenotype and increased IL-10 production",
    "97.3% increase in bone volume fraction compared to unmodified PEEK in osteoporotic rabbit femoral defects"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a mechanism for translating pathological acidity into targeted osteoimmune regulation and strontium delivery, offering a novel strategy for enhancing bone repair in osteoporosis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how human land use affects microplastic accumulation in terrestrial wildlife. Using in vivo microplastic measurements and tracking data from lowland tapirs and giant anteaters across Brazil, researchers found that microplastic burdens increase with human footprint exposure and proximity to development infrastructure. Microplastics were also detected in remote areas, indicating indirect pathways beyond direct human landscape exposure. The findings highlight the interaction between habitat use and land-use change in shaping wildlife microplastic exposure.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Microplastic burdens in free-ranging mammals rise with human footprint exposure and proximity to development infrastructure",
    "Microplastics detected in remote habitats far from development, suggesting indirect exposure pathways",
    "Individual habitat use and anthropogenic land-use change jointly influence wildlife microplastic accumulation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This research identifies critical interactions between human land-use change and wildlife microplastic exposure, providing foundational insights into the ecological mechanisms driving microplastic accumulation in terrestrial ecosystems."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how cooperative multi-hydrogen adsorption dynamically modulates interfacial charge transfer in catalytic systems. Using a sulfur-rich CoReS2+x cocatalyst, the researchers demonstrated that abundant unsaturated sulfur sites facilitate multi-hydrogen adsorption, inducing electron delocalization and creating an electron-deficient inter. This adsorption-driven electronic redistribution reverses electron injection thermodynamics from endothermic to spontaneous, accelerating charge transfer to ultrafast timescales. The system achieves 67.8% apparent quantum efficiency for hydrogen evolution from formic acid.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Cooperative multi-hydrogen adsorption on sulfur-rich CoReS2+x cocatalyst enables dynamic electronic gating",
    "Adsorption-induced electron delocalization creates an electron-deficient interfacial state",
    "The gated interface converts endothermic electron injection into spontaneous transfer with ultrafast kinetics"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes multi-adsorbate-induced electronic gating as a general mechanism for dynamically programming interfacial charge transfer in catalytic and energy-conversion materials under reactive conditions."
}

{
  "summary": "This phase 1b trial assessed neoadjuvant nivolumab and ipilimumab in resectable hepatocellular carcinoma. Patients received the combination before surgery to evaluate safety, immune responses, and tumor microenvironment changes. The regimen was feasible with 91% surgical completion and 25% objective response rate. ctDNA clearance post-surgery correlated with improved recurrence-free survival, while T-cell responses linked to treatment efficacy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Neoadjuvant nivolumab plus ipilimumab was feasible with 91% of patients undergoing surgery",
    "Objective response rate was 25% (8/33 patients) with disease control in 97%",
    "Absence of circulating tumor DNA after surgery predicted 2-year recurrence-free survival"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This study provides early evidence that neoadjuvant immune checkpoint inhibition can modify tumor immunity and predict recurrence in hepatocellular carcinoma, supporting the potential for ctDNA monitoring in post-resection surveillance."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated nuclear physical properties during early mouse embryonic development, focusing on zygotic gene activation. Using direct organelle capture and biophysical methods, researchers discovered transient nuclear softening at the two-cell stage. This softness, driven by rapid lamin B1 clearance via autophagy, enables efficient zygotic gene activation. Nuclei that remain stiff due to lamin B1 retention show incomplete gene activation and developmental failure. The findings highlight a critical physical state necessary for initiating embryonic transcription.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Nuclei exhibit transient softening at the two-cell stage in mice",
    "Lamin B1 clearance via autophagy (LC3) enables nuclear deformability",
    "Nuclear stiffening from lamin B1 retention impairs zygotic gene activation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes a physical mechanism linking nuclear mechanics to zygotic gene activation, revealing how transient nuclear softening facilitates embryonic development."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates stabilizing cobalt-free ultrahigh-nickel layered cathodes by thermodynamically guiding antisite ordering. Using density functional theory, researchers identified tellurium as a dopant that promotes spatially correlated Li/Ni antisite configurations. The synthesized cathode, LiNi0.948Al0.045Te0.007O2, exhibits ordered domains that enhance structural stability, suppress phase transitions, and maintain high capacity retention (96.7% after 200 cycles at 1 C) and Li+ transport. The approach demonstrates defect spatial organization as a key strategy to balance stability and electrochemical performance.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Tellurium doping induces spatially correlated Li/Ni antisite configurations in cobalt-free ultrahigh-nickel cathodes",
    "Ordered domains persist during cycling, suppressing phase separation and c-axis contraction",
    "The cathode maintains 96.7% capacity retention after 200 cycles at 1 C with a 4.4 V cutoff"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes defect spatial organization as a critical design principle for stabilizing high-nickel cathodes without cobalt, addressing the stability-transport trade-off through thermodynamic control of antisite configurations."
}
